items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-85-4.1 Hence $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0 = L$ and $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0 = M$, while $\lim_{x \to 0} f(g(x)) = 1 \ne 0 = M$: the refuted claim is false. [step 1.3, step 3.1, L5]
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-86-
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-87-4.2 Both extra hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fail for the pair $(f, g)$: hypothesis (i) fails because $L = 0$ lies in $B = \mathbb{R}$ while $f(L) = f(0) = 1 \ne 0 = M$, and hypothesis (ii) fails because $g(x) = 0 = L$ for every $x$, so no punctured neighbourhood of $0$ avoids the value $L$. For the pair $(f, \iota)$, hypothesis (ii) does hold with $\eta := 1$, since $\iota(x) = x \ne 0$ whenever $0 < |x - 0| < 1$; and step 3.2 confirms the conclusion of the theorem there. [step 1.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, L6]
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-88-
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md:89:5.1 So the extra hypotheses of the true theorem are not removable, and the obstruction is located exactly at the values of the inner function that equal $L$. [step 4.1, step 4.2] ∎
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-90-
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-91-## Remarks
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-92-
items/cex-composition-of-limits-fails.md-93-- **The same outer function serves both roles.** With $g$ the composition fails, with $\iota$ it succeeds, and $f$ is unchanged. So the failure cannot be attributed to any pathology of $f$ beyond the one recorded in [[cex-limit-differs-from-the-value]]: that its value at $0$ differs from its limit at $0$.
--
