{"id":"ex-rational-function-field-order","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordered_field"],"rationale":"The source gives exactly the eventual-sign/leading-coefficient order on R(t), states that t exceeds every constant, and identifies the field as non-Archimedean. The local proof expands representative independence and the infinitesimal 1/t.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
{"id":"ex-field-ordered-in-two-ways","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The two orders on Q(sqrt(2)) obtained from the two real embeddings are a standard algebraic example, and the local proof also shows these are the only two. Searches surfaced informal exact discussions but no reputable source among the allowed classes for the full exact package; this is established mathematics, not an invented construction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
{"id":"ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: the exact six-row inventory for this library’s K=R((t^{-1})) is a bespoke composition rather than an established named result. The core ordered-field, Cauchy-complete, non-Archimedean and non-LUB assertions follow from source-backed local Laurent results. The unrestricted-interval row was independently checked from its explicit endpoints a_n=n t^(-1), b_n=1/(n+1): nesting is immediate, while a common x would have valuation at least 1 from x<=b_n for all n and valuation below 1 from x>=a_n for all n, a contradiction. The row’s citation to an ai-generated target remains an eligibility defect reported separately.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
{"id":"ex-cesaro-means-of-alternating","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grandi%27s_series"],"rationale":"The sources give Cesaro averaging and the classical alternating pattern. The local item uses the term sequence 1,-1,... rather than Grandi partial sums, changes the Cesaro value accordingly to 0, and writes out the parity computation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
{"id":"ex-stolz-cesaro-applied","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faulhaber%27s_formula","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The local example composes two sourced results rather than reproducing one source statement: Faulhaber’s formula gives leading term n^(p+1)/(p+1), hence the displayed limit, and Stolz-Cesaro supplies the stated difference-quotient route. The local proof expands the power factorization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}

